is eight news now we are in spring break  here for clark county school district  and if you're a ccsd parent and maybe  it's a little bit of a break for you too  getting children up early for school it  is a challenge that's why health experts  are advocating for later school times as  bradley blackburn reports new research  shows it can really benefit the whole  family  [Applause]  high school teacher kelly osuna and her  two teen sons have busy mornings getting  ready for the day but they say it's been  much less stressful since their school  began starting classes later prior to  the time change i had to get them into  before care  and then have enough time to get back to  my job by seven so we were leaving the  house at the very latest  by 6 15. health experts say early school  start times can impact teens learning  and increase risks for obesity and  depression now research from national  jewish health looks at how moving start  times later can be good for the whole  family researchers partnered with a  large district near denver adjusting  middle and high school start times 50 to  70 minutes later what we found was that  these parents were reporting later wake  times similar to their students and an  increase in the amount of sleep that  they were obtaining dr lisa meltzer  authored the study which also found  fewer parents reported feeling tired  during the day researchers say many  middle and high schools begin too early  only about 20 percent of u.s high  schools start at or after 8 30 a.m  that's the time recommended by the  american academy of pediatrics sleep is  important for every aspect of  functioning osuna is grateful to be  getting more sleep she now gets up at  the time she used to leave the house i  noticed such a huge difference  just in  my  mood and my affects when i do get in  front of students in the morning or even  the time that i'm with my children in  the morning and as an educator and mom  she says it's a great way to start the  day  bradley blackburn cbs news
 

